Node Breakdown
1. WhatsApp Trigger
Listens for inbound WhatsApp messages via webhook. Fires on every new message received
by the connected WhatsApp Business account. Passes the full message payload —
including the sender's phone number, display name, and message body — to the agent.
Key design decision: The phone number (wa_id) is used as the session key for
memory, ensuring each user has their own isolated conversation context. Two users
messaging simultaneously never share or contaminate each other's session.

2. AI Agent (Core Orchestrator)
The central node. An n8n LangChain agent that:

Receives the incoming message and user name from the trigger payload
Detects intent and decides whether to show a menu or process a topic selection
Calls the CoachingInfo tool when a topic is identified
Composes and returns a response

Agent system prompt architecture:
The prompt encodes four behavioral layers:
Layer 1 — Persona rules

Respond as a human advisor, not a bot
Use informal "tú" address
No signatures, bullet lists in replies, or code blocks
Default response length: 1–2 sentences + one question or action

Layer 2 — Intent detection logic
Two trigger conditions are defined:

Menu trigger: greetings, vague messages ("I want to improve"), or requests for
options → show the coaching invitation menu once and wait
Selection trigger: any message containing a topic keyword or synonym → treat as
a choice

Layer 3 — Topic normalization
All user inputs are normalized to one of five exact labels before tool-calling:

Enfoque y productividad
Balance de roles y prioridades
Influencia y marca personal
Seguridad psicológica y confianza en equipos
Bienestar integral (sueño, relaciones, propósito, ejercicio)

This normalization step is critical — it ensures the tool query always uses a
standardized key, regardless of how the user phrased their choice ("I want better
sleep" → "Bienestar integral...").
Layer 4 — Fallback behavior
If the tool returns no data, the agent generates a plausible 1-example response from
its own training knowledge, then closes with the standard booking CTA. This prevents
silent failures from surfacing as empty or broken responses.

3. OpenAI Chat Model (GPT-4.1-mini)
The language model powering the agent. GPT-4.1-mini was selected over GPT-4o for
cost efficiency in a high-frequency conversational context — this workflow fires on
every inbound WhatsApp message, so model cost per execution matters significantly
at scale.

4. Simple Memory (Conversation Buffer)
Maintains a sliding window of the last 15 messages per user, keyed by WhatsApp phone
number. This enables the agent to:

Remember which topic the user selected earlier in the conversation
Avoid re-showing the menu to users who already chose
Reference previous context in follow-up responses

Key design decision: 15-message window balances context depth with token cost.
Enough to maintain coherent multi-turn conversations; short enough to avoid excessive
context window usage on long conversations.

5. Coaching & Bienestar (Google Sheets Tool)
A Google Sheets document connected as an AI tool — the agent calls it like a function,
passing a normalized topic label and receiving structured coaching content in return.
Key design decision: Using Google Sheets as the knowledge base (rather than
embedding content in the prompt) has three advantages:

The client can update coaching content without touching the workflow
The prompt stays lean and focused on behavior, not content
Content can be version-controlled and audited independently of the workflow


6. Send WhatsApp Message
Sends the agent's output back to the original sender via WhatsApp Business API,
using the wa_id from the trigger to route the reply correctly.

7. CRM — Google Sheets Append
After every successful reply, appends a row to a separate CRM Google Sheet with:

Date and time
Contact name
Phone number
Email (if available)
Message content

This creates a persistent lead log that the client can use for follow-up, reporting,
and conversion tracking — without any additional CRM infrastructure.
Key design decision: CRM logging happens after the WhatsApp send, not in parallel.
This ensures a record is only created for conversations where a reply was successfully
delivered — avoiding phantom leads from failed executions.

